        "children": [
          {
            "id": "p11-q1-a",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "a",
            "prompt": "$OA^2+OB^2=25$",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "False.",
              "worked": "False. $\\angle OAB=45^\\circ$ from the bearing. In the elevation triangles, $OB=h$ and $OA=h\\cot50^\\circ<h$, so $\\triangle OAB$ is not an isosceles right-angled triangle. In particular, $\\angle AOB\\ne90^\\circ$, so the stated Pythagoras relationship does not hold.",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 6
          },
          {
            "id": "p11-q1-b",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "b",
            "prompt": "$\\angle OAB=45^\\circ$",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "True.",
              "worked": "True. From $A$, $O$ is due west and $B$ is south-west. The angle between west and south-west is $45^\\circ$.",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 6
          },
          {
            "id": "p11-q1-c",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "c",
            "prompt": "$\\angle OBA=45^\\circ$",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "False.",
              "worked": "False. $OB=h$ and $OA=h\\cot50^\\circ<h$, so $OA<OB$. The angle opposite $OA$ is therefore smaller than the angle opposite $OB$: $\\angle OBA<\\angle OAB=45^\\circ$.",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 6
          },
          {
            "id": "p11-q1-d",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "d",
            "prompt": "$OA=h\\cot50^\\circ$",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "True.",
              "worked": "In right-angled $\\triangle AOH$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\tan50^\\circ&=\\frac{h}{OA}\\\\OA&=\\frac{h}{\\tan50^\\circ}\\\\OA&=h\\cot50^\\circ.\\end{align*}$$\nTrue.",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q1-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "$5^2=h^2+(h\\cot50^\\circ)^2-2h^2\\cot50^\\circ\\cos45^\\circ$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False.",
          "worked": "In right-angled $\\triangle BOH$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\tan45^\\circ&=\\frac{h}{OB}\\\\OB&=h.\\end{align*}$$\nThe included angle between $OA$ and $OB$ is $\\angle AOB$, whereas the given $45^\\circ$ horizontal angle is $\\angle OAB$. The cosine rule gives\n$$AB^2=OA^2+OB^2-2(OA)(OB)\\cos\\angle AOB.$$\nFalse.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q1-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "$h^2=5^2+(h\\cot50^\\circ)^2-10h\\cot50^\\circ\\cos45^\\circ$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "Using $OB=h$, $OA=h\\cot50^\\circ$ and $\\angle OAB=45^\\circ$, apply the cosine rule in $\\triangle OAB$:\n$$\\begin{align*}OB^2&=AB^2+OA^2-2(AB)(OA)\\cos\\angle OAB\\\\h^2&=5^2+(h\\cot50^\\circ)^2-2(5)(h\\cot50^\\circ)\\cos45^\\circ\\\\h^2&=5^2+(h\\cot50^\\circ)^2-10h\\cot50^\\circ\\cos45^\\circ.\\end{align*}$$\nTrue.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      }
    ]
